// A TABLE OF RECORDS WRITTEN IN TWO ORDERS, ON FOUR TARGETS (data.md §4.4, §9's
// record-order; ADR 0128).
// inputs: 0 1 4
// expect: 99 307 1685
//
// A record is a function on its labels, so its value is the same whatever
// order its graph is written in: t's records are written y first, u's x
// first, and both are stored as the tuple of their canonical order (x, y). An
// emitter that used written order would store t's components swapped, and
// read them through projections that are canonical: a silent wrong answer on
// every target at once, which agreement alone cannot see. So the answer is
// position-weighted and the difference ordered, and computed by hand first:
//
//   t[i].x = u[i].x = i + 1,  t[i].y = u[i].y = 2i
//   acc = Σ_{i=0..n} (i + 1)·(3·t[i].y + 100 − t[i].x) + 7·(t[i].x − u[i].x) + 1000·(t[i].y − u[i].y)
//       = Σ (i + 1)·(5i + 99)
//   n = 0: 99;  n = 1: 99 + 2·104 = 307;  n = 4: 99 + 208 + 327 + 456 + 595 = 1685
fn run(n ∈ 0..50) (Int) {
    t = array { record { y : k * 2, x : k + 1 } : k ∈ 0..n }
    u = array { record { x : k + 1, y : k * 2 } : k ∈ 0..n }
    loop i = 0, acc = 0 {
        i > n -> acc
        else -> again(i + 1, acc + (i + 1) * (t[i].y * 3 + 100 - t[i].x) + (t[i].x - u[i].x) * 7 + (t[i].y - u[i].y) * 1000)
    }
}
